Here is a Java program that prompts the user to enter two positive integers and displays their greatest common divisor (GCD):

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GCD {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the first positive integer: ");
        int num1 = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter the second positive integer: ");
        int num2 = scanner.nextInt();

        int gcd = calculateGCD(num1, num2);
        System.out.println("The greatest common divisor (GCD) of " + num1 + " and " + num2 + " is: " + gcd);
    }

    public static int calculateGCD(int num1, int num2) {
        while (num2 != 0) {
            int temp = num2;
            num2 = num1 % num2;
            num1 = temp;
        }
        return num1;
    }
}
```

In this program, we use the Euclidean algorithm to calculate the GCD of two numbers. The algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the larger number by the smaller number and taking the remainder until the remainder becomes zero. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD of the two numbers.

The program prompts the user to enter two positive integers using the `Scanner` class. It then calls the `calculateGCD` method to calculate the GCD of the two numbers and displays the result.<|im_end|>

