You can achieve this by using a semaphore. A semaphore is a synchronization primitive that allows a fixed number of threads to access a shared resource. In your case, the shared resource would be the execution of a coroutine.

Here is a simple example of how you can use a Semaphore to limit the number of running coroutines:

```php
use Swoole\Coroutine\Semaphore;
use Swoole\Coroutine\run;

$semaphore = new Semaphore(5);

run(function () use ($semaphore) {
    for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
        go(function () use ($semaphore) {
            $semaphore->wait();
            echo "Coroutine ".$i." is running\n";
            // Add your coroutine code here
            $semaphore->release();
        });
    }
});
```

In this example, a Semaphore is created with a limit of 5. This means that a maximum of 5 coroutines can be running at the same time. When a coroutine starts to run, it calls the `wait()` method of the Semaphore. If the Semaphore has reached its limit, the coroutine will be blocked until the Semaphore's limit is increased (when another coroutine calls `release()`).

After the coroutine has finished its job, it calls the `release()` method of the Semaphore. This increases the Semaphore's limit by 1, allowing another coroutine to start running if it's waiting.

Remember to install the Swoole extension for your PHP version to use the Semaphore and Coroutine classes.<|im_end|>

