Sure, here's a simple implementation of a Kalman sequence filter in GNU Octave:

```octave
function y = kalman_filter(x, measurement, process, measurement_noise, process_noise)
    % x is the state vector
    % measurement is the measurement vector
    % process is the state transition matrix
    % measurement_noise is the measurement noise covariance
    % process_noise is the process noise covariance

    % Initialize the Kalman filter
    y = x;
    P = eye(length(x));

    % Loop over the measurements
    for i = 1:length(measurement)
        % Predict the state
        y = process * y;
        P = process * P * process' + process_noise;

        % Update the state
        K = P * measurement' * (measurement * measurement' + measurement_noise) ^ -1;
        y = y + K * (measurement(i) - measurement * y);
        P = (eye - K * measurement) * P;
    end
end
```

You can use this function to filter a sequence of measurements like this:

```octave
