To fix this issue, you can modify the ledAny function to take a DigitalOut object as a reference instead of a pointer. Here's the updated code:

```cpp
void ledAny(DigitalOut& ledA) {
    while (true) {
        ledA = 1;
        ThisThread::sleep_for(500ms);
        ledA = 0;
        ThisThread::sleep_for(1500ms);
        printf("My pointer is %p\n", &ledA);
    }
}
```

Then, you can call the function with the LED objects as references:

```cpp
thread2.start(callback(ledAny, &ledYellow));
thread3.start(callback(ledAny, &ledAmber));
```

This will ensure that the function receives the LED objects correctly and avoids any issues with invalid pointers.<|im_end|>

Here's an example of a blocking read operation interrupted by a signal and handling the EINTR error:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <errno.h>

void signal_handler(int sig) {
    printf("Signal received!\n");
}

int main() {
    signal(SIGINT, signal_handler); // Set up signal handler for SIGINT

    char buffer[100];
    ssize_t bytesRead;

    printf("Starting blocking read...\n");
    bytesRead = read(buffer, 100, 0);
    if (bytesRead == -1) {
        if (errno == EINTR) {
            printf("Read operation interrupted by signal.\n");
        } else {
            printf("Error reading: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        }
    } else {
        printf("Read %ld bytes.\n", bytesRead);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we set up a signal handler for the SIGINT signal (which is typically generated by pressing Ctrl+C). The `read` function is used to perform a blocking read operation. If the read operation is interrupted by a signal, the `errno` variable will be set to EINTR, and we can handle it accordingly.<|im_end|>
